                <h2>Case Study: The 'Summer of Chaos' for a Tool App Team</h2>
<p>Last summer, a tool app overseas team faced a typical predicament — their ads ran across Meta, Google, and TikTok, covering 23 advertising accounts in total. With a team of 5 people, everyone had access to all accounts.</p>
<p>Seemingly well-organized, but the problems were actually numerous:</p>
<ul>
<li>Budget allocation was purely based on intuition: more budget to whichever account seemed to perform well today, and tomorrow they'd shift resources to whichever platform seemed promising</li>
<li>The same creatives were tested across multiple accounts, with data scattered across various backends — compiling a weekly report took half a day</li>
<li>A Google account was suspended due to an expired credit card, but it took three days before someone noticed, missing the optimal appeal window</li>
<li>Two people simultaneously adjusted bids on the same campaign, causing chaotic bidding logic and doubling that day's CPA</li>
</ul>
<p>Team lead Lao Wang later recalled: "Back then, we were putting out fires every day — whatever account had problems, we'd deal with it. We had no concept of proactive management."</p>
<h2>Problem Analysis: Four Core Issues in Multi-Account Management</h2>
<p>Analyzing this case carefully, the root cause wasn't the number of accounts, but the lack of a clear management framework. Specifically, there were four core issues:</p>
<p><strong>1. No Account Classification, Unclear Priorities</strong></p>
<p>The 23 accounts were treated "equally" — every account could be a "priority account," which meant none received adequate attention. The team's energy was scattered across numerous accounts, with core accounts and experimental accounts competing for resources.</p>
<p><strong>2. Chaotic Permission Management, High Operational Risk</strong></p>
<p>Everyone could operate all accounts — beneficial for "flexibility," but dangerous because no one knew who changed what. When accounts showed abnormalities, there was no way to trace operation records.</p>
<p><strong>3. No Data Aggregation, Reliant on Manual Statistics</strong></p>
<p>Each platform had different report formats and inconsistent data standards. Half of the weekly performance review meeting was spent on data alignment rather than analyzing optimization directions.</p>
<p><strong>4. Lack of Early Warning Mechanisms, Problems Discovered Passively</strong></p>
<p>Account bans, budget depletion, creative fatigue — these problems were only discovered after causing losses. The team was too busy dealing with crises to focus on proactive optimization.</p>
<h2>Diagnosis: Account Tiering is Key to Solving Problems</h2>
<p>Facing these issues, Lao Wang's team decided to introduce a <strong>layered account management system</strong>. The core concept was simple: not all accounts are equally important. They needed to classify accounts based on campaign objectives and risk levels, managing each tier with different resource allocation and oversight intensity.</p>
<p>This diagnosis was based on a straightforward principle — with limited resources, prioritize energy on accounts most likely to generate returns while ensuring high-risk accounts don't cause major problems.</p>
<h2>Actions: Four Steps to Build an Account Tiering System</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Account Classification, Determine Tiering Criteria</h3>
<p>The team classified their 23 accounts based on two dimensions:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Campaign Objective</strong></td>
<td>Primary acquisition accounts, testing/experiment accounts, brand awareness accounts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Risk Level</strong></td>
<td>High-risk (new accounts, new markets), medium-risk (stable campaigns), low-risk (long-term stable accounts)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>After cross-referencing, they obtained a 3×3 matrix, with 23 accounts distributed across 6 different combinations (some combinations were empty). The 4 accounts in "Primary Acquisition + Medium-Risk" were tagged as the core account group.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Permission Tiering, Align Authority with Responsibility</h3>
<p>Based on account importance and operational risk, three permission levels were set:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Admin Permission</strong>: Only held by team lead and Lao Wang — can modify budgets, pause accounts, handle payment issues</li>
<li><strong>Operator Permission</strong>: Held by performance marketers — can adjust bids, replace creatives, create ads</li>
<li><strong>Observer Permission</strong>: Held by new hires and interns — can only view data, cannot operate</li>
</ul>
<p>Permissions were documented in a Notion table. For each new account onboarding, tiering was confirmed first, then permissions were assigned.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Build Data Dashboard, Standardize Data Definitions</h3>
<p>The team integrated with Yiyu's multi-account management tool, aggregating data from all 23 accounts into one dashboard. Key metrics were standardized:</p>
<ul>
<li>"Conversions" across all platforms unified as "first paid event"</li>
<li>Attribution window unified as 7-day click</li>
<li>Budget consumption progress refreshed hourly</li>
</ul>
<p>Every Monday morning, the team lead could view the full picture by opening the dashboard — no more manual aggregation needed.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Set Up Automated Alert Rules</h3>
<p>For high-risk accounts, three types of alerts were configured:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Balance Alert</strong>: Send email and DingTalk notification when account balance falls below 3 days' consumption</li>
<li><strong>Anomaly Alert</strong>: Trigger warning when hourly spend exceeds 200% of daily average</li>
<li><strong>Suspension Alert</strong>: Push notification immediately upon detecting any account status change</li>
</ul>
<p>In the first week this alert system went live, it caught a Google account's payment anomaly in time, avoiding losses from potential account suspension.</p>
<h2>Results: Changes After Three Months</h2>
<p>Three months later, the team conducted a review with the following key data changes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Accounts managed per person</strong>: Increased from 4.6 to 9.2, efficiency doubled</li>
<li><strong>Account anomaly discovery time</strong>: Reduced from average 3 days to 4 hours</li>
<li><strong>Weekly report preparation time</strong>: Reduced from 4 hours to 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Core account group ROAS</strong>: Improved by 35% (partly due to focused attention)</li>
</ul>
<p>Lao Wang said: "The most important change isn't these numbers, but the team shifting from 'firefighting mode' to 'prevention mode.' We finally have time for proactive optimization instead of being pushed by problems every day."</p>
<h2>Your Team's Starting Point</h2>
<p>If your team also manages multiple advertising accounts, you can start with the simplest step: <strong>List all your accounts first, then classify each by campaign objective and risk level.</strong> This action requires no tools — 15 minutes is enough — but it gives you a holistic view of your account assets.</p>
<p>Next, based on the classification results, decide on management frequency, permission allocation, and alert settings for each account. Layered management isn't achieved overnight — it's a gradual iterative process. The key is to start, then optimize.</p>
